  "worked_solution": "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14195873/assets/67c54d0a678e9ea9d02919abacf9b048778de19e3d0367669455439583b7a767.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the manufacturing cost. We can express the manufacturing cost $C$ in terms of $r$ and $h$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\pi rh\\cdot 2.5+\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5\\pi rh+2\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $100\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n100\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constant equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{100}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(r) & =5\\pi r(\\frac{100}{{r}^{2}})+2\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{500\\pi }{r}+2\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $C,$ so we need to differentiate $C$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{500\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+4\\pi r.\n$$\n\n \n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{500\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+4\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 4\\pi r & =\\frac{500\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{3} & =500\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =125 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{125} \\\\ r & =5\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<5$ then ${C}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>5$ then ${C}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=5$ is indeed a minimum of $C(r).$\n\nTherefore, the container with the smallest manufacturing cost has radius $r=5\\,\\text{m}.$",
